<p>
Yesterday I wrote about James Cherti&rsquo;s <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=14148">Org-ibullets package</a>. Today I&rsquo;m writing about another package that he announced at essentially the same time: <a href="https://www.jamescherti.com/emacs-wizard-el/">wizard.el</a>. It&rsquo;s a package of some carefully written Emacs functions dealing mostly with editing and navigation.
</p>
<p>
Cherti describes his functions as using the builtin Emacs functions rather than third party packages or writing complex logic <i>de nuevo</i>. It&rsquo;s surprising how far you can get with just what Emacs provides out of the box.
</p>
<p>
You can look at Cherti&rsquo;s post or <a href="https://github.com/jamescherti/wizard.el/blob/main/wizard.el">the source code</a> for a list of the functions but their broad categories are:
</p>
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>Highlighting functions</li>
<li>Buffer and State Management</li>
<li>Smart Text Manipulations</li>
<li>Search &amp; Replace</li>
<li>Code Navigation</li>
</ul>
<p>
Cherti has more to say about the functions  in those categories so be sure to take a look at his announcement. If one of your goals is to reduce the number of third part dependencies in your configuration, this package may interest you.</p>
